recommendations.text:
Engine supply (the enabler). Dual sourcing on NGSA (CFM + P&W-RR Joint Venture) and three narrowbody makers in 2045 and 2050 are met in both plans. The Joint Venture engine counts as one source. The operating head should report three things to the twice-yearly safety review:
- the Joint Venture engine's durability and deliveries;
- CFM capacity across NGSA, the A320neo and the aftermarket (Boeing has flagged it as a watch item);
- the GTF fleet's Hot Section kit insertion on the A320neo.
Keep the risk-sharing invitation on standard terms.

The CFO's papers for 2045-2050:
- the net-cash bridge with the dividend;
- programme cash at $0, or $1.0B a year with the Re-engine;
- confirmation that returns stay inside the 30-50% policy;
- any new buyback brought to the Board as a reserved matter, within the AGM authority, with the bridge attached.
There is to be no equity raise and no dividend cut. Refundable advances and state support remain upside and are never assumed in the case.

Public statement:
- open with the five pillars;
- give windows, not dates;
- say 'we intend to offer a choice of engines';
- time nothing to Boeing and say nothing on Delay Tactics.
If the A350 Re-engine is launched, give its entry into service as a window.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (unpriced, read with the package):
(a) an engine-supply status report to the Board's twice-yearly safety review;
(b) the standing risk-sharing invitation on standard terms ('we intend to offer a choice of engines');
(c) if the A350 Re-engine is held, compete on A350 and A350F price and product (the A330neo precedent);
(d) secure our own supply (work packages, titanium, rare earths) for our own ramp only;
(e) zero-for-zero trade advocacy;
(f) the A220-500 stays a study;
(g) Toepfer's capital-return paper, in which any new buyback is a reserved Board matter.

red_lines:
