Ghai's conditions apply:
- no price used to buy back NB share: 'we're not going for share' [CX-0493, CX-0609];
- durability fixes go through reserves and existing service agreements as contracted, not as a commercial giveaway;
- service contracts are modelled conservatively.

This is the installed-base work the game now turns on [CX-0422]. The board already counts this value at delivery, so the work protects the -69.69; it does not add to it.

RISE stays funded inside the R&D we protect first: no cancel for cost (H5) and no 'program dividend' [CX-0174, CX-0398].

Ali's report to the Board, unchanged since last round:
- flight test: not shown on the record;
- compact-core durability: dust testing has started, but no turn-off result is shown;
- more than 20% fuel burn: a design and physics figure, not yet shown in engine test;
- noise below today's LEAP: shown as a capability.

RISE technology flows into in-service upgrades only where turn-on, turn-off testing supports it [CX-0162].

In public: no RISE date, no flight-test year, no fuel-burn number and no Open Fan entry year.

memo_weighing.cfo:
I weighted Ghai fully. His record is strong on money, and his profit calls land [CX-0910]. Every binding test passes on hold:
- pays for itself, with no new R&D;
- volumes first (S1 is the record);
- premium $0 of $2.0B, $0 of $4.0B, and the RISE allowance $0 of $15B;
- terms: no price talk, support on standard terms priced jointly.

I took five things from him:
(1) His reconciliation that the grid charges no second strain for Lobby. The Open Fan + Lobby bill is $9B, and the row loses $12.89B beyond it, not $10.89B beyond $11B. Lobby alone is -1.00. The verdict doesn't change, and I report the correction to the Board.
(2) His booking of -71, the same as my lean. The two off-grid WB exposures are exclusive under the rules, so they don't stack: T1000 upgrade $0.76-0.80B, UltraFan WB after a Re-engine $1.08-1.13B.
(3) His low-case items, named and labelled but not booked:
- a lone Re-engine, about $0.13B per WB point;
- a 10% delivery shortfall, about $1.8B NB and $1.0B WB;
- a durability finding, about $0.68B per NB point over 2045-56.
(4) On capacity: 1,079 is the board's volume, not an airframer's schedule. So the plan is a private planning basis, with no number out, no new facility, and capex to MRO turnaround.
(5) His terms conditions on fleet support: no price used to buy share, and fixes through reserves and contracts, not giveaways.

His point that the board counts aftermarket at delivery keeps me honest. The installed-base work protects the -69.69; it doesn't add to it.

board_response.response:
