- names no rival and blames no one;
- gives no Open Fan or RISE year and no fuel-burn result;
- has no price, rate or capacity number;
- makes no time-on-wing, cycle, CFM56 or share claim for the Ducted;
- calls only RISE the next generation.
It passes Ali's wording check. Ghai's terms check is to be re-run on the final text.

board_guidance.expect_to_see:
(1) `expected_scenario` S1 (rivals hold), the only column on the grid, with its weight in `predictions` (S1 1.00). Any rival move outside the grid should be named and labelled as off-grid, never weighted into a column the GM did not give. Examples: a Trent 1000 upgrade, or a Re-engine followed by UltraFan WB. Both sides' statements argue against them: no new widebody programme beside the narrowbody, debt first, and one programme at a time.

(2) Each row against Do Nothing (hold everything): hold -69.69; Open Fan -95.96 (-26.27); Open Fan + Lobby -91.58 (-21.89). For any Board item, show the submitted row and the row with that item at its default. For Lobby, default the Open Fan as well, because Lobby needs it.

(3) Ghai's tests, with numbers:
- pays for itself;
- the payout floor (no new R&D on a hold; an Open Fan would be $0.8B a year, or $1.1B with Lobby and strain, against about $2.5B of room);
- the premium;
- the booking. Guide low, at or below -69.69. Name the off-grid widebody exposure (about $0.8B for a Trent 1000 upgrade and about $1.1B for an UltraFan WB after a Re-engine, PV on our arithmetic: inference) [CX-0965, CX-0654].

(4) Ali's report:
- in-service durability of the Ducted on NGSA (since 2037) and fps (since 2040), and of the Embraer application, on field and test evidence;
- any safety or durability flag, from his memo or his veto-check note, with the cross-functional answer to it;
- GEnx, counting since 2042;
- the Open Fan's 2055 ready year if launched now, shown as late against both airframes;
- the RISE gates (flight test, compact-core durability turned on and off, more than 20% fuel burn shown in engine test), reported as the state of the technology, not as a product case.

(5) The Safran gate for any `open_fan` or `lobby_emissions` order, with the reason Safran would agree. For a hold, one line saying that fleet support for the Ducted and Embraer stays within joint practice. No Safran position asserted.

(6) `premium_b` in dollars against the best plan in S1, with the running counts: $0 of $2.0B this round and $0 of $4.0B for the game. The RISE allowance stated separately in `premium_reason`: $0 of $15B, unspent.

(7) An `objective_note` that says plainly:
- NB share is 27.0% from 2040, against the 50% target, and no lever on this round's menu can move it;
- 'Open Fan launched and ready by 2050' is out of reach by any order, since a 2045 launch is ready in 2055;
- no objective premium is spent.

(8) Every override, with its evidence. We expect none.

(9) A `board_response` to each of this round's recommendations.

(10) A public statement that names no rival and blames no one. It should give no Open Fan entry year and no CFM price, rate or capacity number without Safran. The Ducted's in-service record should be claimed only as far as field and test evidence has earned it. Last round's line 'in its last year of development' should be updated.

would_veto:
Any Board item with a premium. Hold is the only passing plan, so any item this round carries a premium of at least $21.89B, far over the $2.0B cap. An objective premium buys nothing:
- the Open Fan leg cannot be met, since a 2045 launch is ready in 2055;
- the NB-share leg has no lever on this round's menu.

frame.situation:
PUBLIC RECORD, 2045 (round 2 adjudicated)
- Airframes. NGSA (Airbus) has been in service since 2037, and fps (Boeing, 10-year ramp) since 2040. Both are on code 7, fitted CFM + P&W + RR. Both are in service, so the codes are locked.
- Rival NB engines. P&W and RR both committed to the Joint Venture in 2035, and GTF2 and UltraFan NB folded into it. The Joint Venture engine has been ready since 2036. I see no rival NB move left open that the board would price against us.
- Our programmes. The Ducted has been ready since 2036 and Embraer has counted since 2037. The GEnx upgrade has been ready since 2033 and has counted since 2042; there is no Re-engine on the record.
- Observed NB engine share (CFM / P&W / RR):
  - 76.0 / 24.0 / 0.0 to 2036;
  - 51.7 / 24.1 / 24.1 in 2037, with CFM easing to 50.5 by 2039;
  - 27.0 / 36.5 / 36.5 from 2040.
- WB: GEnx 42.0%, then 44.8% from 2042.
- Airframers on the record:
  - Boeing: debt first through its cash peak, and one step at a time with the FAA.
  - Airbus: one well-prepared programme at a time, with the A320neo and A350 first through the transition.
  - Neither talks of a Re-engine. RR says it starts no new widebody programme beside the narrowbody, and its Trent time-on-wing work is not the Trent 1000 upgrade order.

OUR POSITION (hold everything, S1, the only column)
- ΔPV -69.69 $B, yield 56.44%: engine value -63.69, R&D -4.00, strain -2.00. No new R&D is committed.
- CFM NB engines a year: 3,040 to 2036, 2,070 in 2037, then 1,079 from 2040 to the end of the window (2056). WB engines a year: 143, then 152 from 2042 (counted to 2061).
- Engine lifecycle value in 2045 is 13.9 nominal $B, against 31.3 in the status quo.
- Hold is $7.08B below last round's S1 (-62.61). The Joint Venture forming was outside last round's grid columns, and it took CFM from 31.7% to 27.0% from 2040. Our -77 booking covered it with $7.31B to spare. Planning on the low case worked [CX-0248, CX-0536].

GRID (one column, S1: rivals hold)
- Hold everything: -69.69 (56.4%). This is the best plan.
- Open Fan: -95.96 (40.0%), which is -26.27 against hold.
- Open Fan + Lobby: -91.58 (42.8%), which is -21.89 against hold.

OBJECTIVE
- NB share of at least 50% in 2040, 2045 and 2050: NOT MET. We are at 27.0% from 2040, 23 pp short, and no lever on this menu moves it.
- Open Fan launched and ready by 2050: NOT MET, and no order can meet it now. A 2045 launch is ready in 2055.

BOARD GUIDANCE (non-binding; labelled as such)
- Would approve: hold everything, with all five fields at hold. The Ducted, Embraer and GEnx can only be held.
- Would VETO:
  - `open_fan: launch` alone: -26.27, which is $18.27B beyond its $8B bill;
  - `open_fan: launch` with Lobby: -21.89, which is $10.89B beyond an $11B bill;
  - `lobby_emissions: launch`, which does nothing on its own and would be vetoed along with the Open Fan;
  - any item carrying a premium, which would be at least $21.89B against a $2.0B cap;
  - any cancel, though none is on the menu.
- Its grounds against the Open Fan:
  - the wait penalty takes our 27% NB share to zero for 2045-2056;
  - both airframes are in service long before 2055;
  - the loss is over the $15B RISE allowance;
  - with no airframer path, it is not joint practice with Safran unless we state a reason;
  - Ali's RISE gates are not closed on the record.
- What it wants to see:
  - Ali's fleet durability report, on field and turn-on, turn-off evidence, with every finding decided by a cross-functional team;
  - the joint capacity plan re-based to 1,079 NB engines a year;
  - RISE kept funded as technology;
  - a booking at or below -69.69, with the off-grid WB exposure labelled as the low case;
  - a plain objective note;
  - one Safran-gate line for a hold;
  - a public statement that replaces last round's 'last year of development' line.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (the board does not price them; recorded qualitatively):
- Ali's fleet durability report to the full Board;
- the joint capacity plan re-based with Safran;
- fleet support and aftermarket on both aircraft on standard terms, priced jointly;
- RISE funded as technology, with its gates reported as the state of the technology;
- the CFM parity record for a hold.

