options_to_test:
Struck as not legal, listed so the record shows why:
- `gtf2_solo: cancel`: the engine has been ready since 2036, and the cancel window closed in 2035;
- `jv_with_rr: withdraw`: illegal after the 2035 formation;
- `gtf2_solo: launch` or `launch_if_selected`: no live airframe is waiting to enter service;
- `jv_with_rr: commit`: already formed, so it adds nothing.
None appears in the brief's options. The 2035 hold-or-withdraw re-test is therefore closed with nothing left to decide.

Not proposed, tested only to show why:
- a merged derivative or UltraFan NB content insertion into the Joint Venture engine. It is not on the grid, would be a new appropriation with its own return case against a buyback, and would put an in-service engine's configuration at risk. Durability before efficiency [PX-0073][PX-0095];
- any widebody move. It breaks a red line [PX-0027].

red_lines:
The Board's would-veto list, carried in. Nothing this round. Its standing tests apply if any revised brief offered a Board item:
- a cancel or withdraw must beat hold (+8.88) at the weights and not trail it in any plausible column;
- any engine order needs a live airframe that has selected P&W and is not yet in service;
- any P&W cash beyond $2B needs Neil's gate and Finance review with the dividend whole;
- any term that moves the gear or core into the Joint Venture fails.
The acceptable alternative in every case is hold.

other_moves.detail:
Covers our share of the fleet: about 10,233 engines delivered by end-2044 and 1,460 a year from 2040. Each quarter Watson reports:
- durability in severe-environment operation;
- life-limited parts at full declared life;
- the joint support agreement;
- partner-shop induction slots ahead of the first shop-visit wave from the 2037-40 cohorts;
- life-limited-part and hot-section spares;
- spare engines for severe-environment operators.
Retrofit rule if durability bites, on the Trent 1000 model:
(1) the Committee hears first, before anything is said in public;
(2) protect the fleet with inspection intervals and a spare-engine pool;
(3) the design authority issues the kit, inserted at shop visit through the joint partner shops;
(4) the date of anything new moves;
(5) McCabe sets the cost split.
Watson claims no data, signature or shop count the record cannot show.

Narrowbody ramp and early-unit costs for our part, now shared with P&W, measured against:
- her corrected base of about $1.9-4.3B (£1.4-3.1bn) over 2037-44, held at the top of the range;
- a reasonable worst case of about $7.5B (£5.5bn), or about $9B (£6.7bn) if we carry a retrofit alone, against the ~$10B solo case.
The board carries no ramp line, so she reports the range until actuals exist. She also reports:
- the cash freed by the 2035 fold: $2.29B of Solo spend avoided, about £1.7bn;
- the $1.71B write-off, non-cash, about £1.26bn;
- the net LTSA balance movement, shop visits and time on wing, and any onerous-contract provision on early units.
Funding order: the balance sheet first, then the 30-40% dividend, then investment and further distributions. If the ramp runs over, buybacks give way first, and a fleet fix is never re-phased. No equity, leverage below about 1.5x, no borrowing for distributions. Any step beyond buybacks comes to the Board before it arises. Gross R&D stays broadly flat.

would_approve:
Every field at hold. Holds are not Board items and are never vetoed. On the narrowbody fields, `ultrafan_nb_solo` and `jv_with_pw` have only hold this round. The Joint Venture formed in 2035 and stands as approved; the Solo folded into it. Row "UltraFan NB: hold": +32.35 / +32.35 / +32.61 in S1-S3, or +32.38 at the board's illustrative weights (0.80 / 0.10 / 0.10). It is the best grid plan in every column, so no premium. Control's metrics:
- own narrowbody money $5.7B in every column: a breach, sunk and final;
- strain $0;
- RR widebody share 55.2% in 2050;
- RR narrowbody share 36.5% in 2050;
- Airbus widebody airframe share 41.2% / 40.2% / 41.2%.

Other moves we would welcome (read with the package, not voted):
- Trent time on wing and fleet support inside the existing budget: not the `t1000_upgrade` order, and no strain;
- in-service support of the Joint Venture fleet through the joint partner-shop network;
- standard commercial terms with both airframers, through the Investment Committee, with no launch-style concessions.

recommendations.text:
The installed fleet comes first. Watson should report to the Safety, Energy Transition & Tech Committee every quarter on the Joint Venture engine in service: NGSA since 2037, fps since 2040, and about 10,233 engines in our share by end-2044. The report should cover:
- durability in severe-environment operation;
- life-limited parts at full declared life;
- the joint support agreement;
- partner-shop and spares capacity for 1,460 engines a year.
If durability bites, retrofit the fleet first, as we did with the Trent 1000 durability package, and tell the Committee before anything is said in public. Product safety is and always must be our most important priority.

Make the partnership work, and show the board it does. Bring a Joint Venture governance note before the 2050 round. Pratt & Whitney says in public that the venture shares risk and investment, not technology, and that the gear, the core and GTF2 stay its modules, under its design authority. We said we bring a technology. The note should set out:
- design authority;
- our technology role and how it is protected;
- work share and revenue against equal narrowbody shares;
- the joint support network.
We left IAE because the partners were no longer aligned, and we will not drift there again. No Rolls-Royce money beyond our $4B share goes in without the board. Say nothing in public on the venture's terms beyond the record.

levers_in_play:
Other moves, unpriced:
- fleet-first support of the Joint Venture engine;
- Trent time on wing inside the existing budget;
- standard commercial terms with both airframers;
- the Joint Venture governance and alignment note;
- McCabe's ramp-cost funding.

