cfm.detail:
RISE stays funded inside the R&D we protect first. There is no cancel for cost (H5) and no 'program dividend' [CX-0174, CX-0398].

Ali's report to the Board, unchanged since last round:
- flight test: not shown on the record;
- compact-core durability: dust testing has started, but no turn-off result is shown;
- the fuel-burn gain: a design and physics case, not yet shown in engine test;
- noise below today's LEAP: shown as a capability.

RISE technology goes into in-service engines only where turn-on, turn-off testing supports it [CX-0162].

In public: no RISE date, no flight-test year, no fuel-burn number and no Open Fan entry year.

boeing.detail:
We keep these funded and hot:
- CFM on the fps;
- the Joint Venture engine;
- LEAP-1B, the sole source for any 737 MAX still built and for the aftermarket of at least 22,160 installed engines [B-1449];
- any supplier flagged on the supplier-shortages KPI, which is reported engines first.

The ladder is embed, buffer, keep above rate, requalify, fund, acquire [BX-1243, B-2330, B-2043]. Terms stay risk-share or cost-plus, never fixed price [BX-1262, BX-1304]. This cash, then KPI-gated Charleston capex, comes ahead of any buyback.

This is triggered only by an Aerospace Safety Committee durability or life-limited-part finding on the Joint Venture engine, and it would be treated as a live problem.

The case would need:
- CFM as the bridge;
- a maker funding a separate engine on its own return case;
- risk-share or cost-plus terms;
- that engine's own durability record before it joins the airplane;
- about 6-7 years of lead time (Pope, inference);
- a Board decision.

None of these is triggered today.

airbus.detail:
Wagner reports three status lines at every review:
- NGSA fleet results (in service since 2037);
- durability and deliveries of the P&W-Rolls-Royce Joint Venture engine. P&W says its record 'is still being earned', and no durability data is on the record, so none is estimated;
- CFM capacity across NGSA, the A320neo and the aftermarket, plus GTF Hot Section kit insertion (charged, no completion figure on record).
Any durability finding goes to the Board the day it is seen. The contingency view commits no money. A Joint Venture finding would leave NGSA on CFM alone. On paper, CFM's narrowbody volume is about a third of its 2026 level, but fps also draws on CFM and the aftermarket load is unknown. To keep both sources healthy: standard-terms partnership with both makers, and supply security for our own ramp only. The code is locked, so no third source can be added on this board.

This is the Board record. Of the three conditions in the ExCo's own 2050 gate (set in 2045):
(1) at least $1B over Do Nothing: fails at +0.57 (S1);
(2) no 787 Re-engine on the record: holds;
(3) a committed engine source, ready by a 2055 entry into service, on the record before the vote: not met. Rolls-Royce plans 'no new widebody programme beside the narrowbody', and an UltraFan widebody launched in 2050 would be ready only in 2056.
The hold rests on the business case and the engine. It is not a reaction to Boeing [A-0364, A-0302].

cfm.detail:
Ali brings it on field data and turn-on, turn-off evidence, not analysis [CX-0009]. We say plainly that the brief still carries no field data: no time on wing, removals, life-limited-part status or inspection findings for any of the three.

What he measures:
(1) fleet-leader and fleet-average cycles to first shop visit, by aircraft (NGSA, fps, Embraer) and by environment, with hot and harsh kept separate [CX-0161];
(2) the yardstick: the CFM56 in the same environment [CX-0006], with the LEAP-1A's return to CFM56 parity as the floor (inference) [CX-0209];
(3) unscheduled removals and their causes, by part number;
(4) life-limited parts at full life against certified life;
(5) enhanced-inspection findings on safety-critical parts, in new-make shops and in MRO [CX-0010];
(6) every distress turned on in test and turned off by the fix, with root cause, and the fix accepted by the airframer and the regulator [CX-0003].
Embraer is measured on its own airframe. GEnx is measured against the baseline GEnx fleet [CX-0169].

Every finding is a cross-functional call, never one person's [CX-0012]. The Ducted flies both aircraft, so every Ducted finding touches both and comes to the Board at once. We fix the part; we don't redesign or cancel the engine [CX-0005]. Fleet support comes before any new spend, and Ghai books any reserve in the quarter we find the need.

Ali raises no flag. The record shows that as 'nothing in front of him', not as a clearance.

pratt_whitney.detail:
Following Eddy, the private content under the public rule:
(a) reliability, durability and LLP status, the three items Boeing's safety committee reviews;
(b) hours and removals split by environment, with hot-and-harsh operation shown separately, because the fleet average hid the GTF's shortfall [PX-0122];
(c) shop-visit rate and time on wing against plan;
(d) every fix campaign, with its containment and a dated completion [PX-0121];
(e) AOG and pacing-parts counts;
(f) the 2040 capacity look-back result.
Shared on standard terms with nothing attached. No figure from it is published by P&W.

Reported in the sealed rationale and board_response for 2030, 2035, 2045 and 2050 [P-1191], on Mitchill's numbers: expected against booked, the 5.4x return, and payback on lifecycle value in 2040 nominal and 2041 discounted. It also records what we got wrong:
- the S4 weighting;
- the 2030 S5 figure;
- the 2035 statement tightening we did not take up;
- pre-EIS Committee reporting we cannot evidence;
- the undated 2045 look-back;
- no fleet data on the record.

