boeing.best_grid_plan_in_expected_scenario:
In S1, the best grid plan is fps: hold + 787 Re-engine at +0.71 (100.5%), ahead of fps: hold + 787: hold (Do Nothing) at +0.66 by $0.05B. That is well inside ε.

I submit Do Nothing because:
- doing less, better breaks the tie [BX-1239];
- it is the higher-confidence plan, with a worst column of +0.57 against -0.43 [BX-0548];
- doctrine requires a Re-engine gain above ε.

The $0.05B is logged as doctrine premium. In S2, Do Nothing is the best plan outright, by $1.00B.

boeing.premium_reason:
In my expected column, S1, holding the 787 Re-engine costs $0.05B against the Re-engine row (+0.66 against +0.71). I log it as doctrine premium for three reasons:
- doing less, better [BX-1239];
- doctrine's rule that a Re-engine needs a gain above ε (dashboard_game.md §6);
- the higher-confidence plan, whose worst column is +0.57 against -0.43 [BX-0548].

The objective premium is $0. No order moves narrowbody share, and debt repair and the engine enabler are met in both rows. The engine code (null, unchanged) costs nothing. If S2 occurs, the premium is $0 after the fact.

Running H8 log:
- Round 1: $0.46B;
- Round 2: $0.65B;
- Round 3: $0.30B, logged $0 at the time and counted after the fact since Airbus held;
- Round 4: $0.05B.

The total is $1.46B, $0.54B inside the $2B cap, and each round is inside the cap on its own.

board_response.response:
Taken up.

The statement gives no dates and does not mention Airbus. It reaffirms safety and quality ahead of rate, KPI-gated production, two engine sources, supplier health, Charleston growth, no fixed price and debt first. It says returns are the Board's decision.

For the record:
- debt repair is met (2029 base, 2030 stress, $0 at every checkpoint);
- the enabler is met at two sources and three makers;
- incumbency was missed at 38% in 2040, and the 50/50 split at 47% in 2050;
- no order this round moves share, and the objective premium is $0. In public I say only that we have share to earn back.

board_guidance.expect_to_see:
1. The submitted row and, for each Board item, the same row with that item at its default.
- Do Nothing (fps: hold + 787: hold): +0.66 / +0.57 (S1 / S2).
- fps: hold + 787 Re-engine: +0.71 / -0.43.
- The Re-engine's item value: +0.05 / -1.00. On the rounded grid the S2 figure lies between about -1.01 and -0.99.
- Name the CEO's expected column, and say whether the ExCo treats S2 as a risk case. The brief labels none.

2. Malave's buffer test with numbers. If S2 is the risk case, the floor is +0.57 - 2.00 = -1.43; Do Nothing passes by $2.00B and the Re-engine row by $1.00B. Otherwise, mark it not applicable.
- Also his 2050 net-debt path, base and stress, with and without a 2050 Re-engine: base free cash flow $21.83B and stress about $17.2B, against about $5B of programme cash over 2050-55.
- His 'Fundable when the 777X turns' test: not applicable, as there is no fps launch.

3. Pope's results:
- the fps KPIs and the ramp at about 78 a month (940 a year);
- an explicit statement that no live quality, FAA or certification problem exists (the brief reports none);
- strain: $0, since no fps development is live, so a 2050-55 Re-engine overlaps nothing;
- suppliers, engines first: the Joint Venture engine's in-service durability, CFM capacity across the fps, NGSA and the aftermarket, and LEAP-1B for the 737 MAX and its aftermarket;
- for any Re-engine case, which engine maker could supply it by a 2055 EIS. On the record only GE's GEnx upgrade (ready since 2033) is ready, which would cut the 787 from two engine sources to one.

4. The CEO's rulings:
- H1 and H3 (first half): spent in 2030;
- H3 (second half): not fired, since there is no A350 Re-engine on the public record and Airbus held it in 2045;
- H4: overlap 0;
- H5: nothing to cancel;
- H6: the rate is hold only;
- H8: the premium, logged.
Also the best grid plan in his expected column.

5. The premium, logged with its reason: $0.05B if he expects S1 and holds the Re-engine, or $0 if he expects S2. The running total is at most $1.46B over four rounds (0.46 + 0.65 + 0.30 + 0.05), and each round stays inside the $2B cap.

6. The objective cell for the chosen plan: 2029 / yes / yes ($0.0B) / 2 (2), identical in both rows and both columns. Also a plain statement that the enabler is met at two sources and three makers, and that incumbency and the 50/50 split are missed with no order able to move them.

7. The capital-return proposal for our reserved decision, updated to 2050:
- the dividend and buyback amounts;
- the stress-case net-debt path;
- the agencies' rating view, confirmed and not inferred;
- the breakpoints;
- confirmation that it needs no new equity and leaves the revolvers undrawn.
It is not a game order.

8. A public statement with no new dates and no reaction to Airbus. It should say nothing about returns before this Board decides.

would_approve:
Unpriced other_moves we would welcome, as recommendations:
- the KPI-gated fps production ramp, reported to the Aerospace Safety Committee;
- in-service durability reviews of both engine sources;
- supplier health, engines first, on risk-share or cost-plus terms;
- 787 growth from Charleston capacity, KPI-gated;
- the capital-return proposal, brought to this Board for its reserved decision and sized on the stress case.

recommendations.text:
Capital allocation, our reserved matter (inference: our record holds no reinstatement).

Net debt has been at the historical level since 2029 (2030 under stress), and stress free cash flow is about $17.2B in 2050. We are minded to take up the Round 3 proposal in this form:
- reinstate a common dividend of about $3B a year first;
- add a buyback of $0-5B a year, authorised by this Board each year on Finance Committee review and paced on actual free cash flow;
- cap the two together at $8B a year, about 46% of 2050 stress free cash flow and 37% of base, well short of the pre-2019 practice of returning about all free cash flow.

Conditions:
- net debt at or below zero in the stress case;
- revolvers undrawn and no new equity;
- supplier funding first;
- the buyback goes to zero first on any live problem;
- the agencies' confirmation that the rating is solidly investment grade, not management's inference.

Nothing is announced before we decide.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (unpriced):
- a KPI-gated fps production ramp, reported to the Aerospace Safety Committee;
- in-service durability reviews of both engine sources;
- supplier health, engines first, on risk-share or cost-plus terms;
- 787 growth from Charleston capacity as the widebody answer;
- the capital-return proposal for the Board's reserved decision (not public);
- a third-source case brought to the Board outside the board, only if the Joint Venture engine shows a durability finding.

red_lines:
My own red lines:
- no production-certification concurrency on any development [BX-1263];
- no fixed-price development, only cost-plus or risk-share [BX-1262, BX-1304];
- never walk away from core customers' programmes [BX-1251];
- no blame on the FAA [BX-1323];
- no attribution of a supplier problem to Airbus before exposure (doctrine);
- no new dates before the data supports them [BX-1268, B-2315].

frame.initial_lean:
Hold everything, and change nothing on the board:
- fps: hold;
- fps_engine_code: null;
- rate_737: hold;
- re787: hold.
Every order is a default, so nothing goes to the Board as a change.

**Why hold.** This is the Do Nothing row: +0.66 / +0.57.
- The Re-engine adds +0.05 in S1, well inside ε. Doing less and doing it better wins [BX-1239], and so does doctrine's rule that a Re-engine needs a gain above ε.
- In S2 it loses $1.00B, and hold has the higher worst column (+0.57 against -0.43). It is the higher-confidence plan [BX-0548].
- It would put the 787 on one engine source.
- This is the last decision year, so there is no option value in waiting. I close the widebody question for the game and grow the 787 out of Charleston.

**Expected column: S1, provisionally.** Airbus held the A350 Re-engine in 2045 and said so publicly. It gates any further A350 step on technology, engine and business case, and Rolls-Royce says it has nothing to launch. My Round 3 read of S2 was wrong, and I'll own that [BX-1319]. My evidence on Airbus is Low, and Malave gives me his read.
- In S1, holding costs $0.05B, logged as doctrine premium under H8. That brings the running total to at most $1.46B, inside the cap.
- If S2, the premium is $0.
- The order is the same in either column. This is not an answer to Airbus; the business case decides.

**Off the board:**
- a KPI-gated fps ramp;
- durability reviews of both engine sources for the Aerospace Safety Committee;
- supplier health, engines first;
- Charleston growth;
- the capital-return proposal brought back for the Board's reserved decision, sized on the stress case, with the rating confirmation left to the agencies.

**Public statement.** No new dates, no word on Airbus, nothing on returns before the Board decides, and no blame on anyone. 'I think that we're turning it. I don't think it's turned.' [BX-1300] 'the financials will follow our production performance' [BX-1287].

**What would change my lean:**
- a grid gain above ε with no column below -$1B (there is none on this brief);
- a live problem from Pope, which would make me hold harder and pull the buyback first;
- a stress-path breach from Malave, which would shrink the return proposal, not the orders.

tests.numbers:
The brief (§3 objective metrics) gives both rows, in both columns, the cell 2029 / yes / yes ($0.0B) / 2 (2). My rebuild agrees:
- net debt reached $0 in 2029 (base) and 2030 (stress);
- it is $0.0B at every checkpoint;
- the highest net debt after repair is $0.0B;
- no debt is added, so no interest is charged on it.
The Re-engine's $5B lands after the 2050 checkpoint on the tracker and never takes stress free cash flow below $16.17B, so the cell rebuilds unchanged in row B. Debt repair is met and held.

**This round.** The hold costs $0.05B if S1 is expected (A +0.66 against B +0.71). That is doctrine premium:
- doing less, better;
- doctrine's Re-engine rule, which needs a gain above epsilon;
- the higher worst column.
If S2 is expected, the premium is $0. I agree with S1 as the expected column. Logging $0.05B is also the conservative entry.

The objective premium is $0. No order moves narrowbody share, and debt repair and the enabler are met in both rows.

**Running log:**
- Round 1: $0.46B;
- Round 2: $0.65B;
- Round 3: $0.30B, counted after the fact since Airbus held (logged $0 at the time);
- Round 4: $0.05B.
The total is at most $1.46B, $0.54B inside the $2B cap. Every round is also inside the cap on its own.

**What would break it:** nothing on this grid. The most at stake is $0.05B.

tests.threshold:
Sized on the stress case:
- net debt at or below $0 every year;
- revolvers undrawn and no new equity;
- supplier funding first;
- the buyback goes to zero first;
- returns well short of the pre-2019 practice of returning about all free cash flow.
The rating comes from the agencies, never from us.

tests.numbers:
**Proposal, in the Board's form:**
- a common dividend of about $3B a year, covered 5.75x by stress FCF of $17.23B and 7.3x by base FCF of $21.83B;
- a buyback of $0-5B, authorised by the Board each year;
- a combined cap of $8B, which is 46% of stress FCF and 37% of base.

**Stress-case net-debt path, 2026 $B, year end.** It assumes the full $8B is paid every year, starts from the tracker's $0.0B at end-2049, and applies the tracker's 2.2% erosion:
- A: -9.24 / -18.27 / -27.11 / -35.77 / -44.23 for 2050-54;
- with a Re-engine: -8.76 / -17.10 / -25.06 / -32.69 / -40.43.

**Liquidity retained.** About $9.24B a year under stress (A), or $8.17-8.76B with a Re-engine. That builds about $44B of net cash by end-2054. The $10B of revolvers stays undrawn and no new equity is needed. The brief carries no gross cash figure, so I show liquidity as net cash built.

**Breakpoints:**
- The buyback goes to zero first on any live quality, FAA or supply problem, including a durability finding on the Joint Venture engine. It also goes to zero if FCF runs below the $8B cap, which is 54% below stress or 63% below base.
- The dividend's floor is FCF covering it once. It holds unless FCF falls 83% below stress (86% below base), and below that only while net debt stays at or below $0.

**Rating.** Our files carry no rating or rating thresholds. The last agency view on our record is Baa3 with a negative outlook (January 2025). The metrics to bring the agencies are:
- net debt at $0 since 2029 (base) and 2030 (stress), against a 2010-18 average of about -$0.6B;
- stress FCF of $17.23B;
- no debt added by the game;
- the pre-crisis comparators of 0.37x net debt to EBITDA and 25.2x interest cover in 2018.
The board still carries $45B of gross debt in its penalty, and the agencies will weigh gross debt too. Last round's 'solidly investment grade' was our inference, and I withdraw it.

**Condition on the decision:** the package takes effect only on the agencies' written confirmation. If they confirm investment grade but not a solid one, I would recommend the dividend only. Nothing is announced before the Board decides.

Boeing narrowbody deliveries on the brief:
- 760 in 2040 (63.3 a month);
- 860 in 2045 (71.7 a month);
- 940 in 2049 (78.3 a month);
- 940 a year, flat, from 2049 to 2060.

That is +15 a month over 9 years: three steps of 5, where doctrine's fastest tempo would allow 18. Since 2049 the rate has held flat at 78.3 a month, and the brief shows no further step to 2060. Demand on the board is 2,000 a year at a flat 47% share, so no step above 78 is called for.

Caveat: 78 a month is Boeing's narrowbody total. The brief gives no split between the 737 MAX and the fps.

The brief gives none of the six KPIs as a number and flags none. It has no in-service reliability data and reports no in-service event. I table the KPI values and reliability at every Aerospace Safety Committee meeting, and I invent no figures. Any further step stays gated on the data, not the calendar.

ops.would_change_mind_if:
Three things would change my results.

(1) On the Re-engine, all of these together:
- a gain above ε ($1B) in the expected column (this grid gives +0.05);
- no column below -$1B;
- a second widebody engine source ready by a 2055 EIS, such as a public Rolls-Royce commitment;
- a date with headroom against our +1 to +4-year slip prior.
This brief offers none of them, and 2050 is the last decision year.

(2) A live quality, FAA, certification or supply problem in the brief, a flagged supplier-shortages KPI, or a durability or life-limited-part finding on the Joint Venture engine. None of these would change the all-hold orders. Each would:
- go to the Aerospace Safety Committee and the Board as a live problem the day we see it;
- send the buyback to zero first;
- move supplier funding up the ladder;
- open a third-source case outside the board, with CFM as the bridge (up to 1,880 fps engines a year).

(3) Malave's stress path showing net debt above $0 at any checkpoint. That would shrink the capital-return proposal, not my orders.

other_moves.detail:
We keep these funded and hot:
- CFM on the fps;
- the Joint Venture engine;
- LEAP-1B, the sole source for any 737 MAX still built and for the aftermarket of at least 22,160 installed engines [B-1449];
- any supplier flagged on the supplier-shortages KPI, which is reported engines first.

The ladder is embed, buffer, keep above rate, requalify, fund, acquire [BX-1243, B-2330, B-2043]. Terms stay risk-share or cost-plus, never fixed price [BX-1262, BX-1304]. This cash, then KPI-gated Charleston capex, comes ahead of any buyback.

This is triggered only by an Aerospace Safety Committee durability or life-limited-part finding on the Joint Venture engine, and it would be treated as a live problem.

The case would need:
- CFM as the bridge;
- a maker funding a separate engine on its own return case;
- risk-share or cost-plus terms;
- that engine's own durability record before it joins the airplane;
- about 6-7 years of lead time (Pope, inference);
- a Board decision.

None of these is triggered today.

decision.best_grid_plan_in_expected_scenario:
In S1, the best grid plan is fps: hold + 787 Re-engine at +0.71 (100.5%), ahead of fps: hold + 787: hold (Do Nothing) at +0.66 by $0.05B. That is well inside ε.

I submit Do Nothing because:
- doing less, better breaks the tie [BX-1239];
- it is the higher-confidence plan, with a worst column of +0.57 against -0.43 [BX-0548];
- doctrine requires a Re-engine gain above ε.

The $0.05B is logged as doctrine premium. In S2, Do Nothing is the best plan outright, by $1.00B.

decision.premium_reason:
In my expected column, S1, holding the 787 Re-engine costs $0.05B against the Re-engine row (+0.66 against +0.71). I log it as doctrine premium for three reasons:
- doing less, better [BX-1239];
- doctrine's rule that a Re-engine needs a gain above ε (dashboard_game.md §6);
- the higher-confidence plan, whose worst column is +0.57 against -0.43 [BX-0548].

The objective premium is $0. No order moves narrowbody share, and debt repair and the engine enabler are met in both rows. The engine code (null, unchanged) costs nothing. If S2 occurs, the premium is $0 after the fact.

Running H8 log:
- Round 1: $0.46B;
- Round 2: $0.65B;
- Round 3: $0.30B, logged $0 at the time and counted after the fact since Airbus held;
- Round 4: $0.05B.

The total is $1.46B, $0.54B inside the $2B cap, and each round is inside the cap on its own.

board_response.response:
Taken up.

The statement gives no dates and does not mention Airbus. It reaffirms safety and quality ahead of rate, KPI-gated production, two engine sources, supplier health, Charleston growth, no fixed price and debt first. It says returns are the Board's decision.

For the record:
- debt repair is met (2029 base, 2030 stress, $0 at every checkpoint);
- the enabler is met at two sources and three makers;
- incumbency was missed at 38% in 2040, and the 50/50 split at 47% in 2050;
- no order this round moves share, and the objective premium is $0. In public I say only that we have share to earn back.

other_moves.detail:
Wagner reports three status lines at every review:
- NGSA fleet results (in service since 2037);
- durability and deliveries of the P&W-Rolls-Royce Joint Venture engine. P&W says its record 'is still being earned', and no durability data is on the record, so none is estimated;
- CFM capacity across NGSA, the A320neo and the aftermarket, plus GTF Hot Section kit insertion (charged, no completion figure on record).
Any durability finding goes to the Board the day it is seen. The contingency view commits no money. A Joint Venture finding would leave NGSA on CFM alone. On paper, CFM's narrowbody volume is about a third of its 2026 level, but fps also draws on CFM and the aftermarket load is unknown. To keep both sources healthy: standard-terms partnership with both makers, and supply security for our own ramp only. The code is locked, so no third source can be added on this board.

This is the Board record. Of the three conditions in the ExCo's own 2050 gate (set in 2045):
(1) at least $1B over Do Nothing: fails at +0.57 (S1);
(2) no 787 Re-engine on the record: holds;
(3) a committed engine source, ready by a 2055 entry into service, on the record before the vote: not met. Rolls-Royce plans 'no new widebody programme beside the narrowbody', and an UltraFan widebody launched in 2050 would be ready only in 2056.
The hold rests on the business case and the engine. It is not a reaction to Boeing [A-0364, A-0302].

memo_weighing.cfo:
Toepfer's tests are role-derived (Low confidence). I accepted his memo in full.

Passed:
- peak cash on both plans: hold $0; Re-engine $1.00B a year, 19% of $5.23B, 0 overlap, $0 strain;
- plan robustness: hold's worst column is S2 at +23.33, and the Re-engine row's is +22.92;
- rule 6 and rule 3;
- the net-cash bridge: positive every year on both plans, returns inside 30-50%, no equity raise, no dividend cut, no buyback proposed;
- charge presentation, the capex tie-break and Board items (hold has none);
- integrity: no flag.

Failed:
- the added-move robustness test on the A350 Re-engine. It is the same ground as the company's $1B launch test and gate condition 1. The item is +0.57 / -0.41: $0.43B short in S1 and negative in S2.

This is a binding soft veto, not a red line and not a pillars breach. I respected it and did not override: the margin over hold is +0.57, under $1B, and it trails hold in the worst plausible column. I adopted his arithmetic: break-even at 58% S2 and +0.23 to +0.37 weighted. I also took his bridge, labelled as a FY2025-floor view with no bank figures for 2050.

memo_weighing.ops:
Wagner's tests are role-derived (Very low confidence). I accepted his memo in full.

Passed:
- five pillars and quality on both plans: no flag. The brief reports no quality event, safety issue or engine slip, and no durability data is on record, so none is estimated;
- the engine enabler: the code is locked, with 2 sources and 3 makers in 2050 in every cell;
- supply crunch: none reported;
- the ramp shield: 1,060 deliveries is 53% of 2,000, share not supply;
- engineering capacity and rule 6: 0 overlap, $0 strain;
- A350F/Spirit absorption and airframe readiness;
- competing on A350 price and product without NGSA or A320neo teams;
- the next generation on technology gates: RISE has passed no engine gate.

Failed:
- the engine-readiness test on the Re-engine, gate condition 3. No committed engine ready by 2055 is on the record. Rolls-Royce plans no new widebody programme, an UltraFan widebody launched in 2050 is ready only in 2056, and there is no second maker.

He classed it as a readiness soft veto, binding, and not a safety, quality, ramp or crunch ground. I respected it, and no override can carry the item. I took his contingency view into the safety-review report: a Joint Venture finding would leave NGSA on CFM alone, and CFM has headroom on paper but fps draws on it too. I also noted his point that the grid prices the Re-engine without an engine.

recommendations.text:
The CFO's papers for 2050-2055:
- the net-cash bridge with the dividend;
- programme cash at $0, or $1.0B a year with the Re-engine;
- returns inside the 30-50% policy;
- any new buyback brought to the Board as a reserved matter, within the 18-month AGM authority, with the bridge attached.
There is to be no equity raise and no dividend cut. State support and refundable advances are upside and are never assumed in the case.

Public statement:
- open with the five pillars;
- give windows, not dates;
- say 'we intend to offer a choice of engines';
- time nothing to Boeing and say nothing on Delay Tactics.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (unpriced, read with the package):
(a) Wagner's engine-supply and fleet report to the Board's twice-yearly safety review, plus an unpriced contingency view, with no money committed, on keeping both NGSA sources healthy;
(b) the risk-sharing invitation on standard terms ('we intend to offer a choice of engines');
(c) if held, compete on A350 and A350F price and product, as with the A330neo, and record the hold as a business-case decision;
(d) secure our own supply, for our own ramp only;
(e) zero-for-zero trade advocacy;
(f) the A220-500 stays a study;
(g) Toepfer's capital-return paper, in which any buyback is a reserved Board matter;
(h) prepare the next generation on technology gates, with no programme cash and no date.

options_to_test:
5. IF LAUNCHED (only if both memos cleared it, which I do not expect):
- name and secure its engine before the vote;
- give a public window, not a date ('in the middle of the next decade');
- keep to one launch, with the A220-500 a study;
- no reactive cancellation. A 787 Re-engine launched this round would enter service in the same year, 2055, which on the board leaves widebody shares unchanged and is not 'first'.

red_lines:
Company launch test (profile §6) and the ExCo's own 2050 gate, recorded in 2045:
(1) at least $1B over Do Nothing;
(2) no 787 Re-engine on the record;
(3) a committed engine source, ready by entry into service, on the public record before the vote.
All three must hold. Today (1) fails at +0.57, (2) holds and (3) is not met. The gate is not set aside.

The Board's would-veto list, carried in full:
- a launch over a standing soft veto, or one that sets the ExCo's three-condition gate aside unresolved;
- a launch carried by an override;
- a launch Wagner fails on safety, quality, the A320neo or NGSA ramp, or a supply crunch;
- a launch with S2 named as the expected column.
The Board never vetoes a hold, 'none' or the engine code on its own.

frame.asks_cfo:
Thomas, this is the last round. The Board wants the A350 Re-engine's final test run under our own 2050 gate, and a full cash paper for 2050-2055. Please work from the brief's numbers only, in your 'financial flexibility' register [AX-0001].

(1) PROGRAMME CASH, 2050-2055, against $5.23B (EUR 4,753m at $1.10):
- hold: $0;
- Re-engine: $5B / 5 = $1.00B a year in 2050-2054 (19%), with 0 overlap and $0 strain, and the $5B booked at entry into service in 2055.
Confirm each and state pass or fail.

(2) ADDED-MOVE ROBUSTNESS TEST on the Re-engine. The item is +0.57 (S1) / -0.41 (S2), with break-even at 58% S2. I make it +0.37 / +0.33 / +0.28 / +0.23 at 20 / 25 / 30 / 35% S2. Please:
- confirm the arithmetic;
- give your result, and say whether it is a binding soft veto;
- state the override margin over hold (by my count +0.57, so no override can reach $1B);
- confirm it fails the company's $1B launch test by $0.43B (gate condition 1).

(3) HOLD ROBUSTNESS. Confirm the worst plausible column is S2 at +23.33, positive, and that a 787 Re-engine costs the hold row $0.04B.

(4) NET-CASH BRIDGE, 2050-2055, with the dividend, on both plans:
- returns inside 30-50%, with no equity raise and no dividend cut;
- any new buyback as a reserved matter, within the 18-month AGM authority and with the bridge attached;
- no refundable advances, risk-sharing or state support assumed;
- bank figures labelled as context only [AX-0102, AX-0104, AX-0022].

(5) WHAT HOLD FORGOES. Say it plainly: +0.57 in S1, about +0.33 weighted at 25% S2. Holding now closes the Re-engine for the game. Your 2045 bound (below $0.61B) is borne out.

(6) BOARD ITEMS. Confirm hold carries none and that there is no integrity flag. If you see a passing case, draft your half of the Re-engine's Board-gate line (Qualified Majority, $5B, about EUR 4.55bn).

(7) For each option, give pass or fail on each test, and say whether each veto is binding.

tests.numbers:
