other_moves.detail:
Ali brings it on field data and turn-on, turn-off evidence, not analysis [CX-0009]. We say plainly that the brief still carries no field data: no time on wing, removals, life-limited-part status or inspection findings for any of the three.

What he measures:
(1) fleet-leader and fleet-average cycles to first shop visit, by aircraft (NGSA, fps, Embraer) and by environment, with hot and harsh kept separate [CX-0161];
(2) the yardstick: the CFM56 in the same environment [CX-0006], with the LEAP-1A's return to CFM56 parity as the floor (inference) [CX-0209];
(3) unscheduled removals and their causes, by part number;
(4) life-limited parts at full life against certified life;
(5) enhanced-inspection findings on safety-critical parts, in new-make shops and in MRO [CX-0010];
(6) every distress turned on in test and turned off by the fix, with root cause, and the fix accepted by the airframer and the regulator [CX-0003].
Embraer is measured on its own airframe. GEnx is measured against the baseline GEnx fleet [CX-0169].

Every finding is a cross-functional call, never one person's [CX-0012]. The Ducted flies both aircraft, so every Ducted finding touches both and comes to the Board at once. We fix the part; we don't redesign or cancel the engine [CX-0005]. Fleet support comes before any new spend, and Ghai books any reserve in the quarter we find the need.

Ali raises no flag. The record shows that as 'nothing in front of him', not as a clearance.

RISE stays funded inside the R&D we protect first. There is no cancel for cost (H5) and no 'program dividend' [CX-0174, CX-0398].

Ali's report to the Board, unchanged since last round:
- flight test: not shown on the record;
- compact-core durability: dust testing has started, but no turn-off result is shown;
- the fuel-burn gain: a design and physics case, not yet shown in engine test;
- noise below today's LEAP: shown as a capability.

RISE technology goes into in-service engines only where turn-on, turn-off testing supports it [CX-0162].

In public: no RISE date, no flight-test year, no fuel-burn number and no Open Fan entry year.

board_guidance.expect_to_see:
(1) `expected_scenario` S1 (rivals hold), the only column the GM gave, weighted S1 1.00 in `predictions`. Name any rival move outside the grid and label it off-grid; never weight it into a column the GM did not give. The record argues against such moves this round: P&W and RR say there is nothing to launch, RR rules out a new widebody programme beside the narrowbody, Airbus holds the A350 Re-engine, and Boeing starts no new 787 development.

(2) Each row against Do Nothing (hold everything):
- hold: -69.69;
- Open Fan: -86.74 (-17.05);
- Open Fan + Lobby: -85.08 (-15.39); Lobby is +1.66 inside the row, and only against a penalty the Open Fan itself creates.
For any Board item, show the submitted row and the row with that item at its default. For Lobby, default the Open Fan as well. Lobby alone is not on the grid: -1.00 for no board effect, on Ghai's round 3 reading.

(3) Ghai's tests, with numbers:
- pays for itself;
- the payout floor: $0 a year on a hold; an Open Fan would be $0.8B a year, or $0.9B with Lobby, against about $2.5B of room;
- the premium;
- the booking: at or below -69.69. Label the off-grid widebody exposure as the low case. If ordered in 2050, Ghai's round 3 figures were about $0.41B for a Trent 1000 upgrade and about $0.50B for UltraFan WB (inference) [CX-0965, CX-0654].

(4) Ali's report:
- the Ducted's in-service durability on NGSA and fps, and the Embraer application's, on field and test evidence, saying plainly if the brief still has no field data;
- any safety or durability flag, from his memo or his veto-check note, with the cross-functional answer to it;
- GEnx counting since 2042;
- the Open Fan's 2060 ready year if launched now, shown as late against both airframes and after the 2056 NB window;
- the RISE gates (flight test, compact-core turn-off, more than 20% fuel burn shown in engine test), reported as the state of the technology.

(5) The Safran gate for any `open_fan` or `lobby_emissions` order, with the reason Safran would agree. On a hold, one line saying fleet support stays within joint practice. No Safran position asserted.

(6) `premium_b` in dollars against the best plan in S1: $0 of $2.0B this round and $0 of $4.0B for the game. The RISE allowance stated apart in `premium_reason`: $0 of $15B, unspent at the end of the game.

(7) An `objective_note` that says plainly:
- NB share is 27.0% from 2040, against 50%, and no lever on this menu moves it;
- 'Open Fan launched and ready by 2050' cannot be met, since a 2050 launch is ready in 2060;
- no objective premium is spent.

(8) Every override, with its evidence. We expect none.

(9) A `board_response` to each recommendation below.

(10) A public statement that:
- names no rival, the Joint Venture included, and blames no one;
- gives no Open Fan entry year;
- gives no CFM price, rate or capacity number without Safran;
- claims the Ducted's record only as far as field data has earned it;
- takes up Ali's round 3 wording caution on 'on time'.

levers_in_play:
Other moves (the board does not price them; recorded qualitatively):
- Ali's fleet durability report to the full Board;
- customer durability data given through CFM governance, jointly with Safran;
- the joint capacity plan held at the board's volume, with no new facility;
- fleet support and aftermarket on standard terms, priced jointly;
- RISE funded as technology, with its gates reported;
- the CFM parity record for a hold.

